+# Spec Delta
+
+## Purpose
+
+Lets services refer to a value inside a JSON document with a JSON Pointer string, as standardized by RFC 6901, and retrieve that value.
+
+## ADDED Requirements
+
+### Requirement: Pointer syntax follows RFC 6901
+A JSON Pointer SHALL be either the empty string or a sequence of one or more reference tokens, each made of "/" followed by zero or more characters. A string that is not a valid JSON Pointer SHALL be rejected with an error, whether or not the document could have resolved it.
+
+#### Scenario: Pointer without leading slash
+- **WHEN** the pointer is `foo` (or `#/foo`)
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Pointer is not a string
+- **WHEN** the pointer is a value that is not a string
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+### Requirement: Empty pointer refers to the whole document
+The empty pointer SHALL resolve to the entire document, whatever its type.
+
+#### Scenario: Whole document
+- **WHEN** the pointer is `""` and the document is `{"a": 1}`
+- **THEN** the document itself is returned
+
+#### Scenario: Scalar document
+- **WHEN** the pointer is `""` and the document is `null`
+- **THEN** `null` is returned
+
+### Requirement: Reference tokens are unescaped
+Within a reference token, `~1` SHALL stand for `/` and `~0` for `~`, decoded in that order of precedence so that `~01` is the text `~1`. A `~` not followed by `0` or `1` SHALL make the pointer invalid.
+
+#### Scenario: Escaped slash and tilde
+- **WHEN** the document is `{"a/b": 1, "m~n": 2}` and the pointers are `/a~1b` and `/m~0n`
+- **THEN** `1` and `2` are returned respectively
+
+#### Scenario: Escaped tilde followed by one
+- **WHEN** the document is `{"~1": 5}` and the pointer is `/~01`
+- **THEN** `5` is returned
+
+#### Scenario: Invalid escape
+- **WHEN** the pointer is `/a~2b` or `/a~`
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+### Requirement: Object members are selected by name
+A reference token applied to an object SHALL select the member with exactly that name, including the empty name. Only members of the object itself SHALL be selected, never inherited ones.
+
+#### Scenario: Nested members
+- **WHEN** the document is `{"foo": {"bar": [10, 20]}}` and the pointer is `/foo/bar/1`
+- **THEN** `20` is returned
+
+#### Scenario: Empty member name
+- **WHEN** the document is `{"": 0}` and the pointer is `/`
+- **THEN** `0` is returned
+
+#### Scenario: Missing member
+- **WHEN** the document is `{"a": 1}` and the pointer is `/b`
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Inherited property names
+- **WHEN** the document is `{}` and the pointer is `/toString`, `/__proto__` or `/constructor`
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+### Requirement: Array elements are selected by index
+A reference token applied to an array SHALL be a zero-based index written as `0` or as decimal digits with no leading zero, and SHALL select that element. A token that is not such an index, an index past the last element, and the token `-` (which denotes the nonexistent element after the last) SHALL cause an error.
+
+#### Scenario: Valid index
+- **WHEN** the document is `["a", "b"]` and the pointer is `/1`
+- **THEN** `"b"` is returned
+
+#### Scenario: Out of range index
+- **WHEN** the document is `["a", "b"]` and the pointer is `/2`
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Non-canonical index
+- **WHEN** the document is `["a", "b"]` and the pointer is `/01`, `/+1`, `/-1`, `/1.0` or `/length`
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: End-of-array token
+- **WHEN** the document is `["a", "b"]` and the pointer is `/-`
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+### Requirement: Scalars have no children
+A reference token applied to a string, number, boolean or `null` SHALL cause an error. A value that is falsy, such as `0`, `false`, `""` or `null`, SHALL be returned when the pointer refers to it.
+
+#### Scenario: Descending into a scalar
+- **WHEN** the document is `{"a": null}` and the pointer is `/a/b`
+- **THEN** an error is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Falsy value
+- **WHEN** the document is `{"a": null, "b": false}` and the pointers are `/a` and `/b`
+- **THEN** `null` and `false` are returned respectively
